WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.720
The night sky is never truly dark. If you removed light pollution, the moon, stars and galaxies,

00:06.880 --> 00:09.480
there would still be a very faint colourful glow.

00:09.840 --> 00:14.760
That's air glow. With cameras, you can photograph it only on the darkest of nights.

00:15.160 --> 00:23.120
It's about one-tenth as bright as the combined light of all the stars. From above, it forms a luminous bubble encapsulating Earth.

00:24.080 --> 00:30.480
Appearing right at the interface to space, air glow holds clues to how our atmosphere affects weather in space,

00:30.640 --> 00:33.400
and how space weather affects humans on Earth.

00:33.720 --> 00:38.040
The balance of light spanned from 50 to 400 miles above Earth's surface.

00:38.360 --> 00:41.960
In the uppermost boundary of the atmosphere is the ionosphere.

00:42.280 --> 00:45.640
This is where our GPS signals and astronauts travel.

00:46.240 --> 00:50.280
What makes this region complicated is that it's constantly changing.

00:50.840 --> 00:55.920
It reacts to both energy emanating from the sun and weather near Earth's surface.

00:56.280 --> 01:02.160
And as the ionosphere fluctuates, so can conditions in near Earth's space where the space station lies.

01:02.720 --> 01:07.040
But spotting changes in the ionosphere is a lot like trying to watch the wind.

01:07.320 --> 01:10.960
You need a marker of some kind to see the invisible particles move past.

01:11.560 --> 01:13.520
And for that, we have air glow.

01:14.120 --> 01:19.920
These colourful lights reflect changes in the ionosphere, and this is due to the way it's formed.

01:20.400 --> 01:26.080
Our atmosphere consists mainly of nitrogen and oxygen, and small traces of other molecules.

01:26.640 --> 01:30.640
When these molecules reach the upper atmosphere, they're at the mercy of the sun.

01:31.240 --> 01:34.120
Ultraviolet radiation from sunlight excites them.

01:34.480 --> 01:38.520
They become energised and need to release their extra energy in some way.

01:39.280 --> 01:43.640
Atoms that remain energised long enough can emit their extra energy through light.

01:44.080 --> 01:48.400
In the lower atmosphere we don't see as much light, the atmosphere there is dense.

01:48.800 --> 01:53.200
So when an atom becomes energised, there's a high chance it will bump into another atom

01:53.200 --> 01:56.280
and lose energy in that collision instead of emitting light.

01:57.080 --> 02:00.320
But as you travel further up, the atmosphere thins out.

02:00.680 --> 02:04.200
And like a game of dodgeball, the longer atoms stay untouched,

02:04.400 --> 02:08.040
the more time they have to emit a bright colourful photon for us to see.

02:08.400 --> 02:13.920
That's why air glow is only seen in the upper atmosphere, but it can get even more complicated.

02:14.160 --> 02:16.200
Some collisions can produce light too.

02:16.840 --> 02:19.880
On the night side of Earth, green light is the brightest

02:19.880 --> 02:24.960
and occurs when oxygen atoms become excited through collisions with oxygen molecules.

02:25.880 --> 02:29.720
A variety of other complex reactions create red and blue light,

02:29.920 --> 02:33.920
as well as UV and infrared light that are invisible to the human eye.

02:34.560 --> 02:40.440
Each type of air glow contains information about the composition, temperature and density of the upper atmosphere,

02:41.000 --> 02:44.640
all of which are key factors that can change dramatically and rapidly.

02:45.440 --> 02:48.240
So air glow turns out to be a fantastic proxy,

02:48.600 --> 02:51.800
illustrating not only how particles move through the ionosphere,

02:52.160 --> 02:54.480
but what kinds of particles even exist there,

02:54.840 --> 03:00.040
which is key information for helping us tease out how space and Earth's weather interconnect.

03:00.440 --> 03:04.320
And that's a great reason for NASA to study this beautiful phenomenon.

